---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-germany`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Germany',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Germany', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Germany: Winter Deficiency, DGE Guidance, and Dosing"
  description="Germany sits at 47–55°N, so cutaneous vitamin D synthesis stops for roughly half the year. What the DGE and RKI advise, how common deficiency is, and sensible winter dosing."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-germany", label: "Vitamin D in Germany" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Germany</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Germany stretches from about 47°N at the Alpine border to 55°N on the Baltic coast — the same
      band of latitude that gives most of the country a genuine "vitamin D winter." For roughly October
      through March the sun never climbs high enough for the skin to make meaningful vitamin D, so
      whatever you banked in summer has to last, or come from diet and supplements.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How common is deficiency?</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      German population data are unusually good. The Robert Koch Institute's national health survey
      (DEGS) found that a substantial share of adults fall below 30 nmol/L (12 ng/mL) — the severe-
      deficiency threshold — particularly in the winter and spring months, and the great majority are
      below the 50 nmol/L (20 ng/mL) mark that the DGE uses as its target at least part of the year.
      Levels swing markedly with season, tracking the loss of UVB.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What German guidance says</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The German Nutrition Society (Deutsche Gesellschaft für Ernährung, DGE) sets an "estimated value
      for adequate intake" of <strong>800 IU/day (20 µg)</strong> for all ages above one year <em>when
      endogenous synthesis is absent</em> — i.e. as a winter figure — and emphasises that sensible sun
      exposure in summer is the primary source. Infants receive a routine
      <strong>400–500 IU/day</strong> (as vitamin D tablets) in the first year to prevent rickets, a
      long-standing and highly successful German public-health practice.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Diet and the fortification gap</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Unlike the US or Canada, Germany does <em>not</em> broadly fortify milk with vitamin D, so dietary
      intake is low — mostly oily fish, eggs, and some margarine. That places more weight on summer sun
      and on supplements through winter. Practically, an 800 IU/day supplement from about October to
      March is a reasonable default for most adults who are not getting tested, with higher, monitored
      doses for people found to be deficient. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-winter" class="text-amber-700 underline">vitamin D winter</a> and
      <a href="/vitamin-d-dosage" class="text-amber-700 underline">dosage</a> pages.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who should pay extra attention</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Older adults, especially in care homes, and anyone housebound.</li>
      <li>People with darker skin, who need more UVB to make the same vitamin D.</li>
      <li>Those who cover up for cultural reasons or avoid the sun.</li>
      <li>Infants — the routine first-year supplement is important.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Figures reflect DGE/RKI guidance for a general audience.
      Anyone with symptoms or risk factors should ask their Hausarzt about a 25(OH)D test rather than
      guessing at a dose.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-germany" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
